 ## Operating characteristics
-
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is slow, typically taking 10-20 hours on a single GPU for a single scene [S1]. Rendering is also slow, taking ~30 seconds per image [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The MLP weights are small (~5MB), but the training process requires storing intermediate activations for backpropagation, leading to high memory usage during optimization [S5].
-*   **Performance:** On the NeRF Synthetic (Blender) dataset, NeRF achieves a PSNR of ~31.0 dB [S1]. It provides high-quality novel view synthesis but suffers from long training times and slow rendering speeds compared to later methods like Instant-NGP or 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High photorealism, handles complex view-dependent effects (specularity, transparency), and produces smooth continuous representations.
-*   **Limitations:** Slow training and rendering, poor surface reconstruction accuracy (density is not a true SDF), and requires careful tuning of sampling and encoding parameters [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** Training is slow, typically requiring 10–20 hours on a single GPU for bounded scenes (e.g., Blender dataset) [S1][S21, unofficial]. Rendering is also slow, taking ~30 seconds per image at 1080p due to the need to query the MLP hundreds of times per ray [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** The model size is small (~5 MB for weights) [S6, unofficial], but memory usage during training is dominated by the gradient storage for the large number of rays processed per batch [44-radiance-field-optimization.md].
+- **Performance:** Achieves state-of-the-art photorealistic novel view synthesis on bounded scenes (e.g., PSNR ~31.0 dB on Blender) [S1]. Performance degrades on unbounded scenes without modifications (e.g., Mip-NeRF 360) [S1].
+- **Strengths:** High fidelity, handles complex lighting and view-dependent effects (specularity, transparency) [S2, S6, unofficial]. No explicit mesh extraction required.
+- **Limitations:** Slow training and rendering. Poor surface reconstruction accuracy (density field is not a signed distance function) [S1]. Struggles with unbounded scenes and reflective surfaces without specific modifications [S3, unofficial][S5].
 
 ## Background
-
-NeRF was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1][S3, unofficial]. It established the field of neural radiance fields by representing a scene as a continuous 5D function (3D position + 2D viewing direction) mapping to color and density. The method was trained to reproduce input views and rendered novel views through differentiable ray marching. Prior to NeRF, implicit representations struggled with view-dependent effects and high-frequency details. NeRF's key innovations were the asymmetric MLP architecture for view-dependent color and the use of positional encoding combined with hierarchical sampling [S1][S3, unofficial].
+NeRF was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S3, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of Implicit Neural Representations (INRs) and volumetric rendering [S13, unofficial]. The method was developed to address the limitations of explicit 3D representations in novel view synthesis, particularly for complex scenes with view-dependent appearance [S1][S6, unofficial]. It established the field of neural rendering, leading to numerous variants and accelerations [S1].
 
